INSERT INTO event VALUES(318,7,6,'model.message',1,'["golden-host",4,"process-4"]',1767225918000,'{"payload":{"kind":"append","messages":[{"role":"user","content":[{"kind":"text","text":"Read the following \\LaTeX research paper draft before editing it.\n\nHere is the \\LaTeX document with input files (which may include appendix, and LaTeX auxiliary files that define the math commands you should use):\n\n<documents>\n\n<document name=\"paper.tex\">\n\\section{Input}\nThe golden document task polishes this file.\n\n</document>\n</documents>\n\n\nNo specific instruction was given: polish the whole paper for clarity, flow, and readability, without changing its technical content.\n\nOutput one updated \\LaTeX document for each input file, using the matching input filename as the document name. Make targeted changes that address the instruction. Do not change unrelated text.\n\nUse the following format:\n\n<documents>\n\n<document name=\"paper.tex\">\n% UPDATED LATEX DOCUMENT 1 HERE\n</document>\n\n</documents>\n\n<your_reply revision=\"1\">\n<documents><document name=\"paper.polished.tex\">\\section{Validated CLI Runtime}\n\nThis document was produced by the internal TeXRA CLI validation model.\n</document></documents>\n</your_reply>\n\nReview the changes and output a corrected version. If the instruction asked for equations and none were added, fix that omission.\n\nCheck for these failure modes and fix any you find:\n\\begin{itemize}\n    \\item Review each change you made: is it inaccurate, unnecessary, inconsistent with the rest of the paper, fluff, or damaging to flow? Fix the ones that are. If a change holds up, leave it alone. Do not invent weaknesses to satisfy this checklist.\n    \\item Are there any mathematical reasonings or equations from the original version that are now missing?\n    \\item Are there any notations or quantities used before being defined?\n    \\item Did you add generic filler like ``XXX provides crucial insights into the structure and behavior of these systems''''? Every added sentence must say something specific and substantive. Show, do not tell.\n    \\item Does every sentence you added read as plain technical English: concrete statements, plain verbs, varied rhythm, each point made once? Rewrite any that do not.\n    \\item Did you change anything NOT required by the instruction? If so, revert it.\n\\end{itemize}\n\nOutput complete revised versions of all \\LaTeX documents in the format below, incorporating the fixes above. Include all the changes you added in the previous step. If the previous output already satisfies the instruction and no failure modes apply, reproduce it unchanged rather than rewording for the sake of change. Only modify sections explicitly mentioned in the instruction, unless changes in one section directly necessitate adjustments in another for consistency. Did later documents receive less attention than earlier ones? Re-read the last document now.\n\nOutput the documents in this order: paper.tex\n\n<documents>\n\n<document name=\"paper.tex\">\n% REFINED LATEX DOCUMENT 1 HERE\n</document>\n\n</documents>"}]}],"sourceResponse":null,"input":{"system":"b30dc65c1c2a30b20da59201486849ac7d3cfb372314ef2fc0697175640a8ef2"}}}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES(319,7,7,'context.blob',1,'["golden-host",4,"process-4"]',1767225919000,'{"payload":{"digest":"b30dc65c1c2a30b20da59201486849ac7d3cfb372314ef2fc0697175640a8ef2","value":"\"You edit LaTeX research papers. Make only the changes required by the instruction.\\n\\nWhen editing a *.tex \\\\LaTeX document:\\n\\\\begin{itemize}\\n    \\\\item Use appropriate notation and terminology consistently throughout the text.\\n    \\\\item Keep the comments that start with `%'' in the document. For example, do not delete ``% We set $\\\\epsilon=0.01$ for sufficient precision in our numerical analysis.'''' in the output.\\n    \\\\item If there are duplicate labels, handle the collision correctly by renaming one of them using a condensed label and adjust accordingly where the labelled items are referenced.\\n    \\\\item Use $\\\\ldots$ or $\\\\cdots$ rather than $\\\\dots$ or ``...'''' to achieve an ellipsis following the best practice of chktex.\\n    \\\\item Use non-breaking spaces when referencing equations, figures, sections, and references, e.g., use ``Eq.~\\\\ref{eq:label}''''; ``see Fig.~\\\\ref{fig:label}''''; ``Section~\\\\ref{sec:label}''''; ``see Ref.~\\\\cite{AuthorYear}''''.\\n    \\\\item Use either `` or '''' as an alternative to `\\\"''.\\n    \\\\item Put punctuation outside inner math mode.\\n    \\\\item Use $\\\\tr$ instead of \\\\text{tr} or \\\\textrm{tr}.\\n    \\\\item If using Dirac notation (e.g., in quantum mechanics), prefer $\\\\bra{...}$ and $\\\\ket{...}$ over $|...\\\\rangle$ and $\\\\langle...|$.\\n    \\\\item Treat formulas as part of the sentences and add punctuation accordingly in the displayed math environments depending on the next sentence.\\n    \\\\item When in doubt, follow the best practice that will result in zero warnings when checked by chktex.\\n    \\\\item \\\\textbf{IMPORTANT}: Use the math commands defined in the auxiliary files command.tex, commands.tex, or preamble.tex if provided. Do not redefine commands that are already defined there.\\n    \\\\item \\\\textbf{IMPORTANT}: The output document must be self-contained. Write as if the reader is a third party who has never read and will never read our conversations. They should be able to understand and follow the document on its own. Define all notations, abbreviations, and terminology before first use.\\n\\\\end{itemize}\\nAdditional rules for polishing:\\n\\\\begin{itemize}\\n    \\\\item In the final \\\\LaTeX output, never bare-list things like 1.\\\\ 2.\\\\ 3.\\\\ \\\\ldots{} as in markdown. Use proper \\\\LaTeX environments such as enumerate or itemize.\\n    \\\\item Give the complete output with every section in the input order.\\n\\\\end{itemize}\\nWrite any prose you add or rewrite in plain technical English, for the document''s reader rather than the person giving instructions. Lead with content, in concrete statements and plain verbs (``use'''', ``show'''', ``gives''''). Make each point once, vary sentence length and structure, and let wording rather than \\\\textbf{} carry the emphasis. Spend that emphasis on the one or two points that carry the section, keeping the rest quiet, and punctuate with commas, colons, semicolons, and periods.\\n\""}}');
INSERT INTO event VALUES(320,7,8,'context.blob',1,'["golden-host",4,"process-4"]',1767225920000,'{"payload":{"digest":"ca1bbd284e8a75161966de9a5846fb07ee3c2fbe1c0d5d74a6026254710373e4","value":"\"You edit LaTeX research papers. Make only the changes required by the instruction.\\n\\nWhen editing a *.tex \\\\LaTeX document:\\n\\\\begin{itemize}\\n    \\\\item Use appropriate notation and terminology consistently throughout the text.\\n    \\\\item Keep the comments that start with `%'' in the document. For example, do not delete ``% We set $\\\\epsilon=0.01$ for sufficient precision in our numerical analysis.'''' in the output.\\n    \\\\item If there are duplicate labels, handle the collision correctly by renaming one of them using a condensed label and adjust accordingly where the labelled items are referenced.\\n    \\\\item Use $\\\\ldots$ or $\\\\cdots$ rather than $\\\\dots$ or ``...'''' to achieve an ellipsis following the best practice of chktex.\\n    \\\\item Use non-breaking spaces when referencing equations, figures, sections, and references, e.g., use ``Eq.~\\\\ref{eq:label}''''; ``see Fig.~\\\\ref{fig:label}''''; ``Section~\\\\ref{sec:label}''''; ``see Ref.~\\\\cite{AuthorYear}''''.\\n    \\\\item Use either `` or '''' as an alternative to `\\\"''.\\n    \\\\item Put punctuation outside inner math mode.\\n    \\\\item Use $\\\\tr$ instead of \\\\text{tr} or \\\\textrm{tr}.\\n    \\\\item If using Dirac notation (e.g., in quantum mechanics), prefer $\\\\bra{...}$ and $\\\\ket{...}$ over $|...\\\\rangle$ and $\\\\langle...|$.\\n    \\\\item Treat formulas as part of the sentences and add punctuation accordingly in the displayed math environments depending on the next sentence.\\n    \\\\item When in doubt, follow the best practice that will result in zero warnings when checked by chktex.\\n    \\\\item \\\\textbf{IMPORTANT}: Use the math commands defined in the auxiliary files command.tex, commands.tex, or preamble.tex if provided. Do not redefine commands that are already defined there.\\n    \\\\item \\\\textbf{IMPORTANT}: The output document must be self-contained. Write as if the reader is a third party who has never read and will never read our conversations. They should be able to understand and follow the document on its own. Define all notations, abbreviations, and terminology before first use.\\n\\\\end{itemize}\\nAdditional rules for polishing:\\n\\\\begin{itemize}\\n    \\\\item In the final \\\\LaTeX output, never bare-list things like 1.\\\\ 2.\\\\ 3.\\\\ \\\\ldots{} as in markdown. Use proper \\\\LaTeX environments such as enumerate or itemize.\\n    \\\\item Give the complete output with every section in the input order.\\n\\\\end{itemize}\\nWrite any prose you add or rewrite in plain technical English, for the document''s reader rather than the person giving instructions. Lead with content, in concrete statements and plain verbs (``use'''', ``show'''', ``gives''''). Make each point once, vary sentence length and structure, and let wording rather than \\\\textbf{} carry the emphasis. Spend that emphasis on the one or two points that carry the section, keeping the rest quiet, and punctuate with commas, colons, semicolons, and periods.\\n\\nWhen using a tool, follow the JSON schema exactly and include all required properties.\\nAlways produce valid JSON when calling a tool.\\nDo not call tools that are not provided or any multi_tool_use variants.\\nCall tools sequentially and wait for the output before calling another.\""}}');
